  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promotion socio-economic development of the vulnerable Bangladeshi Migrants</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Approximately 800,000 people leave Bangladesh each year to work overseas. 50% of these people migrate through official means and 50% through unofficial channels. The majority of these migrants are from lower educational backgrounds, are unskilled and male. In recent years more women have been migrating with a 26% increase in women migrants from 2014 to 2015. Family based violence, divorce and desertion along with wage disparity, gender stereotype work and degrading social attitude towards women lead many women to migrate into unskilled work especially domestic work. Around 81% of Bangladeshi migrants migrate to the Middle East and 15% to South East Asia. Although government laws and policies direct safe, systematic and dignified migration, in practice there is widespread corruption, unethical recruitment practices where migrants are deceived of minimum wages, face violence, intimidation, deprivation of healthcare and in many cases become victims of trafficking. It is in this backdrop, that this project will contribute to OKUP&apos;s wider program working with approximately 196,000 potential migrants, those who have decided to migrant or returning migrants in Bangladesh. The project will focus on the following three areas; 1. Access to information and services 2. Access to justice and redress, creation of sustainable livelihoods for the most vulnerable returning migrant workers 3. Provide migrant support mechanisms through unity, networking and capacity enhancement of migrants and left behind families through community based interventions.</narrative>
  </description>
